00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:10.370
So like, OK, what is blockchain?

00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:11.750
Because I actually have no idea.

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:13.500
It's a word that gets
thrown around a lot,

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:15.920
especially here in the Bay
Area, as where you are as well.

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:17.820
So like, what is
this thing that we

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:19.070
keep hearing about everywhere?

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:20.420
ALLEN DAY: I would guess
most of the audience

00:07:20.420 --> 00:07:23.000
should be familiar with
something called BitTorrent,

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:24.350
which is protocol [INAUDIBLE].

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:25.587
I see your heads are nodding.

00:07:25.587 --> 00:07:27.920
It's a protocol for a bunch
of peer-to-peer technologies

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:30.330
in general, which is a
type of distributed system.

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:32.330
So these peers get
together, and they're

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:34.760
trying to solve some problem,
like make sure everybody

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:36.440
has the same copy of the file.

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:38.480
Or in the case of
this blockchain,

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:40.370
they're trying to make
sure everybody agrees

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:43.130
that the latest set of
transactions that are being

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:45.492
added on to the
end of a log file

00:07:45.492 --> 00:07:47.450
are agreed upon by
everybody that that's really

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:48.800
what is in the latest block.

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:51.350
So there's some consensus
algorithm, essentially,

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:56.068
being reached by a group of
peers to coordinate work.

00:07:56.068 --> 00:07:57.110
Think about it like that.

00:07:57.110 --> 00:07:59.390
MARK MANDEL: How does
that kind of work?

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:01.010
ALLEN DAY: In terms of when
we talk about data structures,

00:08:01.010 --> 00:08:02.240
that's probably the easiest way.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:02.720
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:05.030
Like, do you and I just chat,
and I'm just like, it's cool,

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:06.050
and you're like,
it's cool, and then

00:08:06.050 --> 00:08:07.383
we just go on our separate ways?

00:08:07.383 --> 00:08:08.990
Or is there some
sort of contract,

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:10.317
or what does that look like?

00:08:10.317 --> 00:08:12.650
ALLEN DAY: So there's many
of these different blockchain

00:08:12.650 --> 00:08:14.540
implementations, but at
the most basic level,

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:17.760
they're using a cryptographic
function, a hashing function,

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:18.260
right?

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:19.880
Where the output
is deterministic

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:22.070
based on the input, but
it's not predictable

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:24.440
because the outputs appear
to be uniformly distributed

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:26.730
relative to whatever
input you get,

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:29.270
which means that for a
given input and output,

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:32.360
a peer on the network can
verify that the output

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:35.190
is what it ought to be
based on the inputs.

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:36.360
You can match them.

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:38.480
But in order to find
a particular output

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:41.460
based on the input is
difficult because the outputs

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:43.460
are uniformly distributed
relative to the input.

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:44.870
It's random, essentially.

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:47.060
OK, so what that
means is that if we

00:08:47.060 --> 00:08:50.510
try to reduce the entropy
of the output, like by--

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:53.500
let's say we have a
bit vector of 256 bits,

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:55.410
and we're trying to
make the first bit zero.

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:56.368
OK, that's really easy.

00:08:56.368 --> 00:08:57.870
That's 50% of the time.

00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:00.560
But if you try to make the first
10 bits zero or the first 100

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:04.580
bits zero, it becomes a lot more
difficult to find that, given

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:06.515
that you have to do a
search basically just

00:09:06.515 --> 00:09:08.000
by throwing darts at a board.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:10.790
Just because you've got
99 zeros in one place,

00:09:10.790 --> 00:09:12.770
you can't move directly
next door and get 100.

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:14.827
This could be back
to one, one zero.

00:09:14.827 --> 00:09:15.660
MARK MANDEL: Got it.

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:17.035
ALLEN DAY: So what
these peers do

00:09:17.035 --> 00:09:19.250
is that they look
at how long it took

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:22.280
to find a solution for a
particular number of zeros

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:25.040
to emit a block in a
particular amount of time.

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:27.560
So it's basic set of some
computing power being consumed

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:30.120
over time, which they
call a hash rate,

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:32.330
so how many of these hashing
functions can be done

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:33.390
[INAUDIBLE] at a time.

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:35.630
And if a solution was
found too quickly,

00:09:35.630 --> 00:09:37.310
then the peers agree
because they're all

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:40.070
sharing the same code base
that the difficulty should

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:40.700
be increased.

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:43.650
We now need to add more
zeros to make it slower.

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:46.010
Or if it was done
too slowly, then we

00:09:46.010 --> 00:09:47.992
make it an easier
problem to solve.

00:09:47.992 --> 00:09:49.950
And so this is how they're
doing the agreement.

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:51.325
Some peers are
doing this hashing

00:09:51.325 --> 00:09:54.540
to search for solutions
to make a block

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:56.020
within a particular
amount of time.

00:09:56.020 --> 00:09:58.530
And they're adjusting the
difficulty more or less

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:02.240
based on how close they are
to the desired amount of time.

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:02.990
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:04.110
ALLEN DAY: OK, so now
let's talk about what

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:05.380
is this block all about.

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:07.680
So what are they
trying to agree about?

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:11.050
The block contains a
set of transactions.

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:14.640
So each of the entities
that is participating

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:16.892
on the-- the thing about
cryptocurrencies now--

00:10:16.892 --> 00:10:18.600
this is to make it
simple, like Bitcoin--

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:20.468
anybody can generate a key pair.

00:10:20.468 --> 00:10:22.260
This is just like the
same kind of key pair

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:26.070
you use for PGP,
or SSH, or SSL--

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:26.580
same thing.

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:28.110
So you generate a key pair.

00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:31.590
And your private key can
produce a public key,

00:10:31.590 --> 00:10:33.265
so that's deterministic.

00:10:33.265 --> 00:10:35.640
You can get the public key
directly from the private key,

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:37.390
but you can't get the
private key directly

00:10:37.390 --> 00:10:38.410
from the public key.

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:40.595
So what you do is
you share activities

00:10:40.595 --> 00:10:42.220
that you're interested
to do by signing

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:44.730
with your private key,
which is then verifiable

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:46.750
that it corresponds
to your public key

00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:48.950
in assigning the transaction
that you want to enter

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:50.820
onto the ledger, this log file.

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:52.440
So the blocks
consist of a series

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:54.630
of transactions
that are essentially

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:56.070
entries in a log file.

00:10:56.070 --> 00:10:58.620
All the records, which are
these transfers of value,

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:00.660
like moving Bitcoin
from party A to party B,

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:02.400
those transfer
operations are all

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:04.200
grouped together in a block.

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:05.610
And the block is a transaction.

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:08.120
Like in SQL, where you
say begin and then commit,

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:09.480
the commit is like that.

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:12.030
So all the transfers
within the same block

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:13.060
have the same timestamp.

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:14.580
MARK MANDEL: So very much
like if I was in a bank

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:15.510
and I was like, I
want to move money

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:18.238
from here to here, and between
two accounts, that's why

00:11:18.238 --> 00:11:19.530
you need multiple transactions.

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:20.790
Well, essentially,
it's one transaction

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:22.600
wherein one account
is removing something,

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:24.100
and one account is
adding something,

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:25.140
but at the same time.

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:25.710
ALLEN DAY: Correct.

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:26.940
MARK MANDEL: But in a way that--

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:27.450
ALLEN DAY: It's [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:28.450
MARK MANDEL: --there's
a distributed,

00:11:28.450 --> 00:11:30.510
I'm guessing it's like a
peer-to-peer system that

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:32.502
is able to say, OK, I
know that this happened,

00:11:32.502 --> 00:11:33.960
and we all know
that this happened.

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:35.550
And we can verify
this at a later date

00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:36.440
that we all agree on.

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:36.690
ALLEN DAY: Yeah.

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:37.830
And so there's an
interesting bit here.

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:39.940
So I was talking about
hash rates earlier,

00:11:39.940 --> 00:11:42.000
and so the incentive
for these computers

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:44.220
to be doing the hashing
is that what they will

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:46.650
do as part of their
attempt to find

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:49.500
a solution to this hashing
problem on a block, right,

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:53.130
is they will include their own
transaction to give themselves

00:11:53.130 --> 00:11:55.990
new Bitcoins as
part of that block.

00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:58.950
So if they produce
the block, their block

00:11:58.950 --> 00:12:02.130
contains a block reward that
produces new Bitcoins that

00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:04.740
go to the beta of the
block that it's then

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:06.400
broadcasting onto the network.

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:09.780
And so that particular transfer
is a special type of transfer

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:11.280
called a Coinbase
transaction, which

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:12.822
is where if you've
heard of Coinbase,

00:12:12.822 --> 00:12:14.890
which is the biggest
exchange in the US for doing

00:12:14.890 --> 00:12:16.260
cryptocurrency activity.

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:17.760
CARTER MORGAN: And
so the idea there

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:19.380
is to incentivize
people, allowing

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:21.780
you to use their computers to
form these hash operations?

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:22.863
ALLEN DAY: That's correct.

00:12:22.863 --> 00:12:24.840
Yeah, because you can
mint new cryptocurrency

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:26.538
by doing the hashing.

00:12:26.538 --> 00:12:27.580
CARTER MORGAN: Very cool.

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:29.705
So I got a question where
you were saying basically

00:12:29.705 --> 00:12:32.070
before, we talked
about cryptocurrency.

00:12:32.070 --> 00:12:33.990
Before that, we were
talking about basically

00:12:33.990 --> 00:12:34.675
streaming video.

00:12:34.675 --> 00:12:36.300
And you said that
you have a background

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:38.340
in biomedical and agricultural.

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:40.117
So can you use this
for that as well?

00:12:40.117 --> 00:12:41.700
Are there applications
in those areas?

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:44.670
ALLEN DAY: Yes, there are
some open source projects

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:47.160
in this blockchain
space that are working

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:49.890
on streaming video applications,
something like a CDM,

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:52.022
as well as doing distributed
file storage, which

00:12:52.022 --> 00:12:54.480
would be the equivalent of,
like, fully peer-to-peer Google

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:55.680
Cloud Storage equivalent.

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:58.860
Biomedically, but there
are some companies

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:03.247
that are working on making
data ownerable by the user.

00:13:03.247 --> 00:13:05.580
So if your data are encrypted
according to a private key

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:10.320
that only you control, then you
can decide who has access to it

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:12.660
at what level and at what
time and can revoke access

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:14.790
based on your encryption
of your own data.

00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:16.170
CARTER MORGAN: Oh, that is cool.

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:17.760
ALLEN DAY: Yeah, there there's
various ways you can do this.

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:19.830
Creating new keys and
recrypting or doing

00:13:19.830 --> 00:13:22.770
multi-signature keys where
more than one party has

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:25.210
to sign in order to get
access to some data,

00:13:25.210 --> 00:13:26.960
this is also possible.

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:29.400
Yeah, so there's
applications like that.

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:32.460
For analytical applications,
which is where a lot of what

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:35.900
GCP is doing for
biomedical kind of things,

00:13:35.900 --> 00:13:40.180
there's not really any good way
to partition the computing--

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:42.610
to shard it, is what these
distributed systems people call

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:43.110
it--

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:46.260
to make the computations
cost effective relative

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:47.502
to a centralized cloud.

00:13:47.502 --> 00:13:48.960
And so it would be
for doing, like,

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:51.390
a deep learning
model of retinopathy,

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:53.730
or a breast cancer detection,
or something like that.

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:55.890
It's just not really a
tractable problem right now,

00:13:55.890 --> 00:13:57.810
given the architecture of
the peer-to-peer systems.

00:13:57.810 --> 00:13:59.190
MARK MANDEL: Well, it sounds
like, then, that there's

00:13:59.190 --> 00:14:01.500
actually a wide variety of
use cases for blockchain

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:03.630
outside of cryptocurrency,
is what I'm hearing.

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:04.200
ALLEN DAY: It's true.

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:04.500
Yeah.

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:06.770
Any place where you want to
have immutable data where

00:14:06.770 --> 00:14:08.520
you can prove that was
produced and nobody

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:10.860
tampered with it, that's a
good place where you want

00:14:10.860 --> 00:14:12.268
to use some of this technology.

00:14:12.268 --> 00:14:14.310
So with all this fake news
stuff that's going on,

00:14:14.310 --> 00:14:16.305
like wow, all really
good applications.

00:14:16.305 --> 00:14:18.180
CARTER MORGAN: So then,
I mean, if there's so

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:20.640
many use cases for this,
it's kind of strange

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:22.540
because I've heard
of blockchain a lot,

00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:23.640
and people joke about it.

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:26.280
Like, every startup has a new
blockchain or cryptocurrency

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:26.910
offering.

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:28.535
And so why is it that
this is something

00:14:28.535 --> 00:14:29.980
that people take
light of so much,

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:32.880
something people
make so much fun of?

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:35.040
ALLEN DAY: Yeah,
it got a bad rap.

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:36.790
Kind of a couple
of dynamics here.

00:14:36.790 --> 00:14:39.030
So Bitcoin is the granddaddy
of all of these things,

00:14:39.030 --> 00:14:41.340
and the Bitcoin
community enthusiasts

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:43.170
have their own
strong culture that

00:14:43.170 --> 00:14:45.870
has some political ideology
associated with it.

00:14:45.870 --> 00:14:49.710
It's very similar to the gold
bugs, where there's, like,

00:14:49.710 --> 00:14:51.810
finite supply, and
you can't inflate it.

00:14:51.810 --> 00:14:57.690
And it runs contrary to how
mainstream macroeconomics work

00:14:57.690 --> 00:14:59.260
and how central banks operate.

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:02.520
And so it's a different
kind of thing.

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:04.740
And because it looks
different, it gets ridiculed.

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:07.350
The other aspect of
this is that Bitcoin

00:15:07.350 --> 00:15:09.570
has been having some
success, and Ethereum

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:11.190
has been having
some success, which

00:15:11.190 --> 00:15:13.860
is another cryptocurrency
and smart contact

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:15.370
platform we can talk about.

00:15:15.370 --> 00:15:17.790
But as a result of that, on
Ethereum, you can actually

00:15:17.790 --> 00:15:21.090
mint different types of tokens,
which are not a first class

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:23.100
cryptocurrency in
their own right,

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:25.705
but they can have
cryptocurrency-like attributes

00:15:25.705 --> 00:15:27.330
in that they can be
created and traded.

00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:31.080
And so because there were
enough users using this thing,

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:33.000
some clever
marketers figured out

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:36.720
they could start doing purely
financial and marketing

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:39.810
projects that didn't have
much technical basis or merit

00:15:39.810 --> 00:15:41.640
for solving any problems.

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:45.870
They could just create a token
and ICO it, and buy a Lambo.

00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:48.870
And there was a bunch of
that that happened in 2017,

00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:51.040
and a lot of people
got burned by that.

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:53.400
So that's another reason
that things are ridiculed.

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:54.030
MARK MANDEL: That's fun.

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:55.822
Definitely, I'm in the
same camp as Carter,

00:15:55.822 --> 00:15:58.410
where I've been told, if you
want 5% extra evaluation,

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:00.863
make sure that blockchain's
in your startup title.

00:16:00.863 --> 00:16:01.530
ALLEN DAY: Yeah.

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:04.197
There's some good-- maybe we can
link to this in the description

00:16:04.197 --> 00:16:06.643
about one of these ice tea
manufacturers calling them,

00:16:06.643 --> 00:16:09.060
like, blockchain iced tea, and
the stock went up overnight

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:10.330
by double digit percent.

00:16:10.330 --> 00:16:12.830
MARK MANDEL: Getting back to
more functional type of things,

00:16:12.830 --> 00:16:15.330
so we talked a little bit about
what blockchain is good at--

00:16:15.330 --> 00:16:17.622
and maybe it's also kind of
relevant since we're saying

00:16:17.622 --> 00:16:19.622
people feel like they can
apply it to anything--

00:16:19.622 --> 00:16:20.940
but what is blockchain bad at?

00:16:20.940 --> 00:16:22.032
What's it not good for?

00:16:22.032 --> 00:16:23.490
I've heard of people
saying things,

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:25.290
like, oh, we're going to use
blockchain for databases.

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:26.500
Is that a terrible idea?

00:16:26.500 --> 00:16:27.220
I have no idea.

00:16:27.220 --> 00:16:29.580
ALLEN DAY: It's
an OK idea if you

00:16:29.580 --> 00:16:32.010
want an append-only database.

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:33.690
So basically all of
these things boil

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:38.910
down to being log files that
have some mechanism of reaching

00:16:38.910 --> 00:16:42.060
consensus about what the
contents of the log file are,

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:45.660
using a group of peers that
may completely not trust

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:48.210
one another or may have
some level of trust

00:16:48.210 --> 00:16:49.240
between one another.

00:16:49.240 --> 00:16:50.160
MARK MANDEL: And I'm
guessing that trust is

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:51.470
sort of implementation detail--

00:16:51.470 --> 00:16:52.380
ALLEN DAY: That's correct, yeah.

00:16:52.380 --> 00:16:52.740
MARK MANDEL: --depending
on how you set it up.

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:54.157
ALLEN DAY: How you
implement that,

00:16:54.157 --> 00:16:56.520
and where you have a public
chain or a permission chain,

00:16:56.520 --> 00:16:57.790
it's also related to trust.

00:16:57.790 --> 00:16:58.110
And so yeah.

00:16:58.110 --> 00:16:59.460
So if you have a
situation where you

00:16:59.460 --> 00:17:01.380
need a log file that
can't be tampered with,

00:17:01.380 --> 00:17:02.287
you have a use case.

00:17:02.287 --> 00:17:04.079
But if you want to do
something like search

00:17:04.079 --> 00:17:06.569
the log file to
find old records,

00:17:06.569 --> 00:17:09.270
it's not a use case for that
because it's really just adding

00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:10.750
things onto the end of a list.

00:17:10.750 --> 00:17:13.500
That's really what
the primary concern

00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:17.200
is of one of these consensus
transaction heading systems.

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:19.599
MARK MANDEL: So I'm thinking
through communication type

00:17:19.599 --> 00:17:20.099
stuff.

00:17:20.099 --> 00:17:21.060
Like, I send you a message.

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:22.143
You know it comes from me.

00:17:22.143 --> 00:17:24.687
You know the messages came in
the order that they came in.

00:17:24.687 --> 00:17:25.520
That would be a fit?

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:26.839
ALLEN DAY: That
would be a fit, yeah.

00:17:26.839 --> 00:17:28.800
But realize that every
one of these messages

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:31.080
has to be added into something
that all the peers are

00:17:31.080 --> 00:17:31.900
validating.

00:17:31.900 --> 00:17:34.780
So it could be that there
is some throughput problems

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:35.280
with this.

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:38.400
It's more and more I/O. So this
is another scaling difficulty

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:39.160
of these systems.

00:17:39.160 --> 00:17:41.160
There are some ways
around it using things

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:43.830
like zero knowledge proofs
and other cryptographic stuff,

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:46.460
but that's outside the scope
of what we'll talk about today.

00:17:46.460 --> 00:17:48.360
And it's not clear
that that will even

00:17:48.360 --> 00:17:51.172
be deployable into production
at large scale anyway.

00:17:51.172 --> 00:17:52.630
But I want to go
back to something.

00:17:52.630 --> 00:17:53.850
So I was talking
about like, OK, it's

00:17:53.850 --> 00:17:56.250
really good for adding entries
to the log file, right?

00:17:56.250 --> 00:17:57.690
Putting my data
science hat on, I

00:17:57.690 --> 00:17:59.940
was looking for some kind
of thing I could write about

00:17:59.940 --> 00:18:02.400
as a developer advocate to get
people excited about working

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:03.210
on Google Cloud.

00:18:03.210 --> 00:18:05.400
And I wanted to do some
network analysis of how

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:07.710
all these parties are
interacting with one another

00:18:07.710 --> 00:18:10.290
on the Bitcoin blockchain, or
any blockchain for that matter.

00:18:10.290 --> 00:18:12.600
Who is sending money to
whom, and when, and how much,

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:15.213
and can we figure
anything out about it?

00:18:15.213 --> 00:18:16.380
Are there any patterns here?

00:18:16.380 --> 00:18:17.670
Can we identify exchanges?

00:18:17.670 --> 00:18:21.810
Can we identify big owners, high
throughput nodes, et cetera?

00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:24.480
These type of questions are
very similar to the kind

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:27.300
of questions that I was
addressing in my dissertation

00:18:27.300 --> 00:18:31.260
work because genetic regulatory
networks have the same kind

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:32.260
of characteristics.

00:18:32.260 --> 00:18:34.830
So there's some
information in the form

00:18:34.830 --> 00:18:38.610
of structured molecules coming
into a cell that are food,

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:41.490
basically, or parts
to help the cell run.

00:18:41.490 --> 00:18:44.037
And then there's
some waste molecules

00:18:44.037 --> 00:18:45.120
that are being thrown out.

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:46.537
And then everything
in between has

00:18:46.537 --> 00:18:49.440
to do with a bunch of proteins
and other molecules that

00:18:49.440 --> 00:18:54.150
are parts of the cell that allow
that highly structured material

00:18:54.150 --> 00:18:56.700
to keep the cell running
and turn into the exhaust.

00:18:56.700 --> 00:18:58.470
And so how does that
regulation work?

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:00.630
Well, the way that
we go about analyzing

00:19:00.630 --> 00:19:02.560
it is by collecting
some data using

00:19:02.560 --> 00:19:05.220
a biochemical assay of some
sort, typically very high

00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:06.240
throughput one.

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:08.010
And then we look at
the data over time

00:19:08.010 --> 00:19:09.400
to see what's going on.

00:19:09.400 --> 00:19:13.260
And that type of analysis
requires all this network

00:19:13.260 --> 00:19:16.170
analysis methodology, which
could be directly applied

00:19:16.170 --> 00:19:18.168
to crypto economic networks.

00:19:18.168 --> 00:19:19.710
I'm thinking, OK,
this would be great

00:19:19.710 --> 00:19:21.130
if I can just take some
of these things I already

00:19:21.130 --> 00:19:23.730
know how to do and apply it
to this other data set that's

00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:25.420
getting a lot of
attention right now.

00:19:25.420 --> 00:19:27.960
Because nobody's talking
about economic network

00:19:27.960 --> 00:19:29.250
analysis of these systems.

00:19:29.250 --> 00:19:32.185
They're just talking
about ICOing--

00:19:32.185 --> 00:19:33.060
making tokens, right?

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:34.602
MARK MANDEL: What
does ICO stand for?

00:19:34.602 --> 00:19:36.960
ALLEN DAY: Initial Coin
Offering-- it's like an IPO.

00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:37.350
MARK MANDEL: Got it.

00:19:37.350 --> 00:19:38.975
ALLEN DAY: Yeah, so
I tried to do this.

00:19:38.975 --> 00:19:41.280
And because I
didn't know anything

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:43.777
about any of this stuff
about a year and a half ago,

00:19:43.777 --> 00:19:46.110
so I tried to go and get the
data from the Bitcoin node.

00:19:46.110 --> 00:19:48.068
And I was like, well,
why can't I get the data?

00:19:48.068 --> 00:19:50.670
Why can't I query it so that I
can do an aggregation over time

00:19:50.670 --> 00:19:52.930
to look at what is the volume
of transaction over time?

00:19:52.930 --> 00:19:54.013
There's no way to do this.

00:19:54.013 --> 00:19:56.430
So I was like, OK, well,
I guess I can ETL this out

00:19:56.430 --> 00:19:58.080
into BigQuery and query it.

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:01.260
And so that took a lot more time
than I thought it would take

00:20:01.260 --> 00:20:04.800
and justified writing a
blog post about just doing

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:06.930
that, and giving that
quote away because it

00:20:06.930 --> 00:20:07.930
was really hard to do.

00:20:07.930 --> 00:20:11.310
So I did that, and everybody
was very happy about it

00:20:11.310 --> 00:20:12.690
and started using the data set.

00:20:12.690 --> 00:20:14.440
I got a lot of traction
on our public data

00:20:14.440 --> 00:20:15.730
set program as a result.

00:20:15.730 --> 00:20:16.950
And so because of
that, I haven't really

00:20:16.950 --> 00:20:17.790
done any analysis.

00:20:17.790 --> 00:20:19.290
I work with a bunch
of analysts now.

00:20:19.290 --> 00:20:21.540
But mostly, I'm focused on
building the infrastructure

00:20:21.540 --> 00:20:22.860
to support other analysts.

00:20:22.860 --> 00:20:23.735
CARTER MORGAN: Right.

00:20:23.735 --> 00:20:24.660
OK, now, I'm curious.

00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:27.038
GCP specifically--
what kind of things

00:20:27.038 --> 00:20:28.330
do we have that help with that?

00:20:28.330 --> 00:20:30.038
You said you did
some ETL analysis,

00:20:30.038 --> 00:20:32.580
so that sounds like Stackdriver
or something like that maybe.

00:20:32.580 --> 00:20:34.890
ALLEN DAY: Yeah, so how we
have things set up right now--

00:20:34.890 --> 00:20:37.348
and this is all in GitHub if
anybody wants to grab the code

00:20:37.348 --> 00:20:39.995
to replicate this in
Amazon or wherever else,

00:20:39.995 --> 00:20:41.620
it'll all work--
we're using Kubernetes

00:20:41.620 --> 00:20:43.090
to run the blockchain nodes.

00:20:43.090 --> 00:20:45.760
And we have health checks
and load balances in place

00:20:45.760 --> 00:20:48.000
so that if one of
our peers falls over,

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:49.500
we failover to the other one.

00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:53.100
And then there is a
[INAUDIBLE] composer service

00:20:53.100 --> 00:20:56.580
that is holding the
blockchain nodes periodically

00:20:56.580 --> 00:20:58.170
to get data out.

00:20:58.170 --> 00:21:01.470
When a new block shows up, we
stick it into a Pub/Sub topic.

00:21:01.470 --> 00:21:02.730
We publish to the topic.

00:21:02.730 --> 00:21:05.520
And then there's a
subscriber to the topic that

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:07.320
streams the data into BigQuery.

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:10.613
So we make the BigQuery tables
available to users to query.

00:21:10.613 --> 00:21:12.030
But if they also
want to subscribe

00:21:12.030 --> 00:21:15.000
to the stream of events,
those topics are also public.

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:18.690
So people who want to do real
time trading based on events

00:21:18.690 --> 00:21:20.790
that Google is
publishing to the topic,

00:21:20.790 --> 00:21:22.380
they could do that
if they want to.

00:21:22.380 --> 00:21:23.910
MARK MANDEL: Just so I'm
clear as off the nodes,

00:21:23.910 --> 00:21:25.493
how are they getting
that information?

00:21:25.493 --> 00:21:27.910
Is that, like, a centralized
repository of some kind, or--

00:21:27.910 --> 00:21:29.580
ALLEN DAY: The ones that
we're running on Kubernetes?

00:21:29.580 --> 00:21:30.330
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:32.670
ALLEN DAY: So when you
turn on the Bitcoin node,

00:21:32.670 --> 00:21:34.372
it connects to its
peer-to-peer network.

00:21:34.372 --> 00:21:35.330
MARK MANDEL: Oh, I see.

00:21:35.330 --> 00:21:37.030
ALLEN DAY: So it's a
bootstrapping process.

00:21:37.030 --> 00:21:37.830
MARK MANDEL: Right, got it.

00:21:37.830 --> 00:21:39.860
So you sort of become one
with the mesh network.

00:21:39.860 --> 00:21:41.200
ALLEN DAY: You become
one of the peers.

00:21:41.200 --> 00:21:42.658
And all the peers
start sending you

00:21:42.658 --> 00:21:45.637
old data until you're fully
synced with all the peers.

00:21:45.637 --> 00:21:47.220
And then if you want
to, you can start

00:21:47.220 --> 00:21:49.008
producing new blocks by mining.

00:21:49.008 --> 00:21:50.300
We don't do any mining, though.

00:21:50.300 --> 00:21:53.820
We are just receiving and
transmitting existing chain

00:21:53.820 --> 00:21:56.070
data to be a good citizen
on the peer-to-peer network,

00:21:56.070 --> 00:21:58.112
but we're not actually
mining any cryptocurrency.

00:21:58.112 --> 00:22:00.112
CARTER MORGAN: I need to
look this up right now.

00:22:00.112 --> 00:22:00.990
That sounds so cool.

00:22:00.990 --> 00:22:03.210
ALLEN DAY: Not every day, but
it's more than once a week,

00:22:03.210 --> 00:22:04.680
I get pinged by
somebody who's done

00:22:04.680 --> 00:22:06.388
something interesting
with this data set,

00:22:06.388 --> 00:22:07.340
some kind of analysis.

00:22:07.340 --> 00:22:07.530
MARK MANDEL: Well, yeah.

00:22:07.530 --> 00:22:09.380
What sort of interesting
things have people found?

00:22:09.380 --> 00:22:10.963
ALLEN DAY: The most
interesting things

00:22:10.963 --> 00:22:14.790
are related to basically
characterizing entities

00:22:14.790 --> 00:22:15.640
on the network.

00:22:15.640 --> 00:22:17.880
So it's pseudonymous, right?

00:22:17.880 --> 00:22:19.980
So any given actor
on the network

00:22:19.980 --> 00:22:21.768
has a private key
that's private,

00:22:21.768 --> 00:22:23.310
and then they're
signing transactions

00:22:23.310 --> 00:22:25.930
so you can verify what
their public identity is.

00:22:25.930 --> 00:22:29.790
But beyond that, you have
no idea how any given actor

00:22:29.790 --> 00:22:31.680
corresponds to any other actor.

00:22:31.680 --> 00:22:36.100
But if you start looking at
patterns of activity over time,

00:22:36.100 --> 00:22:38.940
you can begin to get
some sense of what

00:22:38.940 --> 00:22:41.250
attributes could belong
to a particular actor

00:22:41.250 --> 00:22:42.340
on the network.

00:22:42.340 --> 00:22:45.330
Let's say there's a node that's
receiving a lot of inputs

00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:47.130
from many different
other addresses

00:22:47.130 --> 00:22:49.080
and is outputting to
many other addresses.

00:22:49.080 --> 00:22:52.770
This is some kind of flow node
that has a high throughput.

00:22:52.770 --> 00:22:55.080
It's typically an exchange
where people are going

00:22:55.080 --> 00:22:57.030
to trade tokens, for example.

00:22:57.030 --> 00:22:59.367
Yeah, so this is the direction
we're heading right now.

00:22:59.367 --> 00:23:01.200
So we've made the
BigQuery tables available.

00:23:01.200 --> 00:23:02.170
That was the first thing.

00:23:02.170 --> 00:23:04.200
And we've done this for
eight different blockchains

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:04.825
at this point--

00:23:04.825 --> 00:23:06.580
Bitcoin, Ethereum, a
bunch of other ones.

00:23:06.580 --> 00:23:08.220
Then we made the
Pub/Sub topics available

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:09.570
so people can subscribe to that.

00:23:09.570 --> 00:23:11.910
I'm now working on
two additional fronts.

00:23:11.910 --> 00:23:16.110
So one of them is data flows
where the Pub/Sub topics will

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:19.230
be read by a data
flow and output

00:23:19.230 --> 00:23:21.653
to another Pub/Sub topic
for producing derivatives.

00:23:21.653 --> 00:23:23.070
So we could basically
do some kind

00:23:23.070 --> 00:23:25.530
of sliding window analysis,
run it all the time,

00:23:25.530 --> 00:23:28.230
and just publish to another
topic periodically for making,

00:23:28.230 --> 00:23:30.030
like, a candlestick
chart, if you

00:23:30.030 --> 00:23:33.540
to see something like amount of
activity on network over time.

00:23:33.540 --> 00:23:36.150
The other one is really to what
we were just talking about,

00:23:36.150 --> 00:23:39.150
analyzing the network, possibly
also in real time, where

00:23:39.150 --> 00:23:41.220
we can find anomalous
transactions,

00:23:41.220 --> 00:23:45.660
or find labels on entities
that are changing,

00:23:45.660 --> 00:23:48.000
or the entity has some
significant volume through it.

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:51.360
And these type of events of
turning that continuous data

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:53.400
into discrete data
are actually a way

00:23:53.400 --> 00:23:56.310
to take the graph, the
crypto and economic graph,

00:23:56.310 --> 00:23:57.610
and vectorize it.

00:23:57.610 --> 00:23:59.430
So now we have a
vectorized graph,

00:23:59.430 --> 00:24:01.598
and we can start
applying standard machine

00:24:01.598 --> 00:24:03.390
learning models onto
this because we're now

00:24:03.390 --> 00:24:05.640
working in a vector
space as opposed

00:24:05.640 --> 00:24:07.740
to a high dimensional
graph space.

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:10.410
So I'm planning on putting
all that in Bigtable

00:24:10.410 --> 00:24:14.480
and doing cool TensorFlow
stuff on top of that also

00:24:14.480 --> 00:24:16.248
inside of DataFlow.

00:24:16.248 --> 00:24:17.040
